The follower's once-a-second drift check compared the live player
position against the START of the word the JS thread had last heard
about. That thread learns of a commit late by however long it was busy
re-rendering the reel — tens to hundreds of ms during playback — so its
own lateness read as "voice ahead", the check hard-seeked the audio back
to the word start, and the listener heard the word again: about once a
second, every seven to ten words. Every WPM tap seeked unconditionally on
top, so changing speed made it worse.

Now:
- useReelEngine exposes readClock(): the committed index and the frame
  loop's accumulator in ONE synchronous UI-runtime read (runOnUISync), so
  the follower's target is "word start + how long the reel has actually
  shown it", immune to JS-thread lag. No new shared value, no change to
  the frame worklet.
- narrationSync.ts holds the pure math. Drift inside 0.8 s is corrected by
  a pitch-corrected rate nudge, capped at ±15%: a proportional term (0.6 of
  the drift per second, in recording seconds so the loop gain is the same
  at every rate) plus a slow integral that learns the reel's steady
  frame-quantized lag as a bias, so the voice settles ON the reel. Gains
  are soft on purpose — ExoPlayer applies a new rate only once its
  processed audio drains — and a snappier pair rings under that delay.
  Hard seeks remain for real jumps (scrub, sentence skip, word tap: any
  non-±1 index step) and gross drift.
- A WPM change re-rates the player and lets the next check absorb the
  fresh-dwell offset from the engine's accumulator reset; it never seeks.
- Resuming from pause seeks while still paused (silent), so a scrub made
  while the voice sat paused lands exactly.
- The rate floor drops from 0.5× to 0.25× (ExoPlayer allows 0.1×): at 100
  WPM on a ~233 WPM voice the player used to pin at 0.5× and run 16% ahead
  of the reel, which the old check rewound every couple of seconds.
- One in-flight download per segment (services/narration.ts) and one
  prefetch request per segment: the boundary and the 80% prefetch used to
  re-fetch the same 3 MB file on every word until it landed. A failed
  segment load retries after 3 s instead of on every word.

scripts/test-narration-sync.mjs (now part of npm test) pins the math: the
late-listener scenario that reproduces the old bug, the controller's
sign, cap, floor, anti-windup, and closed-loop runs — 60 Hz and 30 Hz reel
lag, the 2.0× ceiling, 0.43×, a WPM tap mid-word, a lag change mid-read —
that must never earn a seek and must settle under 60 ms.

On the phone (0.9.2): narrate at 300–390 WPM for a few minutes — no
repeated words; tap WPM up and down repeatedly — the voice re-paces
without replaying the word; scrub, skip a sentence, pause and resume —
one clean re-land each. Typecheck and npm test are green; device sync is
Michael's to confirm by version stamp.

